\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Transported subdivision has an exact curvature defect}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Subdivide a cellular torus dyadically as in
\ref{fdip-00OW}. Declare every fine link
on the torus with periods #{2N_i}. The coarse link in direction
#{i} at #{x} is the ordered forward product of the two fine
links from #{2x} to #{2x+2\varepsilon_i}. Let #{T_i} denote
fine inverse pullback and let #{\operatorname{ev}} evaluate a
fine coefficient at #{2x}. For an increasing direction set
#{I}, put
##{S_I=\prod_{i\in I}^{\mathrm{increasing}}(\bbI+T_i),\qquad
(s_U^*\alpha)_I=\operatorname{ev}(S_I\alpha_I).}
For the empty set this is evaluation. The products transport
each fine subcell coefficient back along its ordered path.}

\p{The full cochain defect is
##{\begin{aligned}
&\extd_{\mathrm{coarse}}s_U^*\alpha
-s_U^*\extd_{\mathrm{fine}}\alpha\\
&=\sum_{I,\ l\notin I}(e_l\wedge e_I)\otimes
\operatorname{ev}\left(
\bigl((T_l^2-\bbI)S_I-S_{I\cup\{l\}}(T_l-\bbI)\bigr)
\alpha_I\right).
\end{aligned}}
It is zero on degree-zero inputs for every connection, and
zero in every degree when the fine represented connection is
flat. In two dimensions its degree-one coefficient is
##{\operatorname{ev}\left(
(\bbI+T_1)[T_1,T_2]\alpha_2-[T_2^2,T_1]\alpha_1
\right).}
All fine links, including interior square links, are part of
the data; coarse factorization alone does not determine them.}

\proof{
\p{The coarse inverse pullback on an evaluated coefficient
is #{\operatorname{ev}T_l^2}. Thus differentiating the
subdivision sum gives the first operator in the full defect.
Differentiating first inserts #{e_l} and then sums fine
subcells in directions #{I\cup\{l\}}, giving the second
operator. Sum over all pairs #{(l,I)} with their exterior
factors; several pairs may contribute to one exterior
monomial. For #{I=\varnothing}, the difference is zero by
#{T_l^2-\bbI=(\bbI+T_l)(T_l-\bbI)}. Under flatness,
\ref{fdip-00SL} makes all #{T_i} commute, so the same
factorization cancels every term.}

\p{In two dimensions the #{\alpha_2} contribution factors as
#{(\bbI+T_1)[T_1,T_2]}. For #{\alpha_1}, moving
#{e_2\wedge e_1} to positive orientation gives
#{-[T_2^2,T_1]}. These are the two stated terms.}
}

\p{For a nonzero defect over #{\mathbb Z}, use the fine
#{4\times4} sign torus with all links #{1} except
#{U_1(0,0)=-1}, and form the coarse #{2\times2} links by
the prescribed products. Set #{\alpha_1=0} and let
#{\alpha_2} be #{1} at #{(1,1)} and zero elsewhere. At
#{(0,0)}, the two paths in #{[T_1,T_2]\alpha_2} have values
#{-1} and #{1}; their difference is #{-2}. The additional
#{T_1}-shifted commutator is zero because its endpoint is not
#{(1,1)}. The defect at the coarse origin is therefore #{-2}.}

\p{The untransported sum can fail already in one dimension.
Take a fine period-#{4} circle with successive links
#{-1,1,1,1} and vertex values #{0,0,1,0}. Across the first
coarse edge, the two ordinary-summed covariant differences
give #{1}, whereas the coarse covariant difference is #{-1}.
At trivial links, the cup-product and counting-adjoint
failures in \ref{fdip-00OZ} still apply. Objectwise cochain
maps therefore do not by themselves supply a compatible
product, adjoint or inverse-limit operator.}
